    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people clean a deck by pointing a pressure washer at it. That removes dirt and leaves everything else — the mildew spores, the grey oxidised surface, and now a layer of raised, furry wood fibres that drink stain unevenly.
        </p>

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Is Actually On the Deck
          </h2>

          <p>
            Deck discolouration comes in four varieties, and they respond to different chemistry. Identifying yours takes a minute and saves buying the wrong product.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What You See</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What It Is</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What Removes It</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Silver-grey boards</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">UV-degraded surface fibres</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Percarbonate cleaner, then brightener</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Green film</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Algae, usually in shade</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Percarbonate cleaner and a brush</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Black spotting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mildew or mould</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Percarbonate; hypochlorite for heavy cases</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark streaks near fasteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tannin or iron staining</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Oxalic acid brightener</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Flaking, patchy finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Failed film coating</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A stripper, not a cleaner</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            That last row is the one that catches people out. No cleaner removes a failed film finish; it needs a stripper and a different afternoon, which our guide to <a href="/how-to-strip-deck-stain/">stripping deck stain</a> covers. If patches of your deck are peeling rather than worn, read that before buying a cleaner.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tannin streaks in the fourth row are also worth calling out, because they are the one problem a cleaner makes worse rather than better. Alkaline cleaners darken tannin-rich woods like cedar and redwood; only the acidic brightener step brings them back.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cleaners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Choosing the Cleaner
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Percarbonate (Oxygen Bleach) — the Default</h3>
          <p>
            Sodium percarbonate dissolves into hydrogen peroxide and soda ash in water, lifting grey, dirt, algae and mildew without attacking the wood's lignin. It is what most commercial deck cleaners are built on, it does not kill nearby planting, and it will not corrode fasteners. For nine decks out of ten this is the correct and complete answer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sodium Hypochlorite (Chlorine Bleach) — Spot Use Only</h3>
          <p>
            It kills mould faster than anything else, which is why professional cleaners still carry it. It also degrades lignin, leaves the surface furry, corrodes hardware over years and burns planting. Used diluted on a stubborn black patch and rinsed hard, it is a reasonable tool. Used across a whole cedar deck, it is why some decks look fuzzy and pale for years afterwards.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Oxalic Acid Brightener — Not Optional Before Staining</h3>
          <p>
            Not a cleaner at all. It neutralises the alkalinity left behind by the cleaning step, removes iron and tannin darkening, and restores the wood's natural colour. Skipping it is the most common reason a properly cleaned deck still takes stain unevenly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dish Soap and a Brush — for Routine Cleaning</h3>
          <p>
            For a mid-season wash of a deck with a sound finish on it, warm water with a little mild detergent and a stiff-bristle brush is entirely adequate and will not touch the coating. Save the percarbonate for the annual clean and for prep before staining.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whichever you buy, check the label for how much area a container covers and how long it needs to dwell. Those two numbers determine whether you need one jug or three, and our <a href="/best-deck-cleaner/">tested deck cleaner picks</a> compare them directly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="steps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Cleaning, Step by Step
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">Plan the Day</p>
            <p class="text-gray-700 m-0">Work on an overcast, still day if you can. Direct sun dries the cleaner before it has finished working, which is the difference between a deck that comes clean in one pass and one that needs three. Early morning or late afternoon is second best.</p>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Clear and Sweep</h3>
          <p>
            Furniture, planters and grills off the deck entirely. Sweep, then run a putty knife or a purpose-made gap tool down the joints between boards — the packed debris in there holds water against the wood and is where mildew starts and where rot eventually follows. This ten-minute job does more for a deck's lifespan than the cleaning itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wet Everything, Including the Plants</h3>
          <p>
            Soak the deck boards with plain water first: dry wood absorbs cleaner unevenly and blotches. Then thoroughly wet every shrub, bed and lawn area around and below the deck, and keep wetting them through the job. Diluted runoff on wet foliage is largely harmless; concentrated cleaner on dry leaves is not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Apply and Let It Dwell</h3>
          <p>
            Apply with a pump sprayer or a roller, working in sections you can manage in ten minutes — usually 100 to 150 square feet. Let it dwell for the time on the label, typically 10 to 15 minutes, and mist it with water if the surface starts to dry. A cleaner that dries on the deck stops working and leaves streaks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Scrub With the Grain</h3>
          <p>
            A stiff nylon brush on a pole, worked along the boards rather than across them. This is where the grey actually comes off, and it is the step most people skip in favour of more pressure. Pay attention to the shaded edges, the bottom of the stair treads and the area under where the table sits.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rinse Thoroughly, Then Rinse Again</h3>
          <p>
            Every trace of cleaner has to come off, including what has run down into the joints and onto the framing below. Residue left behind interferes with the brightener and, later, with the stain. Rinse the surrounding planting down as well while you are there.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pressure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Using a Pressure Washer Without Ruining the Boards
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pressure washer is a rinsing tool on a deck, not a cleaning tool. Used that way it saves an hour; used as the primary cleaner it permanently damages softwood, and the damage only becomes obvious once the stain goes on and the furred areas drink twice as much.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Settings That Do Not Damage Wood</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">500–1,200 psi on softwood, 25° or 40° tip, 10–12 inches back, always moving with the grain.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Hardwoods tolerate up to about 1,500 psi. Never use a 0° or 15° tip on decking, never hold the wand still, and never let the spray dwell at a board end. Test on a hidden section — under the stairs is ideal — before touching the main floor.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            The tell-tale sign of over-pressuring is a wavy, striped pattern in the boards that follows the arc of the wand. It cannot be washed out; it has to be sanded out, which is a different weekend entirely. If you see it starting, drop the pressure or switch to a wider tip immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            You do not need a pressure washer at all. A garden hose with a firm nozzle, a pump sprayer for the cleaner and a stiff pole brush will clean any deck perfectly well. It takes perhaps an hour longer and carries none of the risk, which for a first-time deck cleaner is a good trade. If you are renting one anyway, our guide to <a href="/pressure-washer-nozzle-colors-explained/">pressure washer nozzle colours</a> covers which tip is which.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brightener" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Brightener Step Nobody Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            After cleaning, the wood surface is alkaline. Cedar and redwood in particular come out of the process looking darker and slightly muddy, and people often assume the cleaner did not work. It did — the colour is a pH effect, and an acidic brightener reverses it in about fifteen minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Apply oxalic acid brightener to the still-damp deck, let it work for the labelled time, and rinse. Three things happen at once: the pH returns to neutral, iron and tannin darkening around fasteners lifts, and the grain opens up so the wood accepts stain more readily and more evenly.
          </p>

          <p>
            That last effect is the reason this step matters even to people who do not care about colour. A cleaned-but-unbrightened deck accepts less stain, and accepts it inconsistently between the sun-bleached and sheltered areas. If a previous staining job came out patchy for no obvious reason, a skipped brightener is the most likely culprit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="before-staining" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cleaning a Deck Before Staining
          </h2>

          <p>
            The cleaning is the same; what changes is that the drying and the final surface now genuinely matter. Three additional rules apply:
          </p>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Brighten, without exception.</strong> The stain's evenness depends on it more than on anything else you do that weekend.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Allow 24 to 48 hours of drying</strong>, and up to four days in humid or shaded conditions. The deck feeling dry underfoot is not the same as the wood being dry.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Test with water, not with your eyes.</strong> Sprinkle water on several boards. Soaking in and darkening means ready; beading means there is still moisture in the surface and stain will sit on top of it.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            Whether to sand afterwards depends on how the boards feel. Cleaning raises grain slightly and pressure washing raises it a lot, so run a hand across a few boards once dry. Smooth means stain away; rough or splintery means a light pass with 60 to 80 grit first, which our guide to <a href="/do-you-need-to-sand-a-deck-before-staining/">sanding a deck before staining</a> covers in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            From there, the rest of the prep — masking, checking the forecast, setting popped nails — is covered in <a href="/how-to-prep-a-deck-for-staining/">how to prep a deck for staining</a>, and the staining itself in <a href="/how-to-stain-a-deck/">how to stain a deck</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="composite" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Composite and PVC Decks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Composite cleans easily and damages easily, which is an awkward combination. Use a cleaner labelled composite-safe, a soft or medium brush, and plenty of rinsing. Percarbonate cleaners are generally fine; anything abrasive or strongly acidic is not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep pressure well down — most manufacturers cap it around 1,500 psi with a wide fan held back — because erosion marks on composite are permanent. There is no grain to sand back and no realistic repair short of replacing the board.
          </p>

          <p>
            Black spotting on composite is nearly always surface mould growing on organic debris trapped in the embossed grain rather than anything happening to the board itself. Clean the debris out of the grain and the spotting stops returning. And if the reason you are cleaning is that the colour looks tired, read <a href="/can-you-stain-composite-decking/">whether composite decking can be stained</a> before reaching for a coating — the cleaning is often the whole fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="stains" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Specific Marks and Spills
          </h2>

          <ul class="space-y-2 my-6">
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Grease and barbecue splatter.</strong> A degreasing detergent while the mark is fresh. Old grease that has soaked into bare wood usually needs sanding.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Rust runs from fasteners and furniture.</strong> Oxalic acid brightener, applied directly to the stain rather than the whole board.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Leaf and tannin marks.</strong> Also oxalic acid. Wet leaves left over winter leave a surprisingly dark print that no amount of scrubbing removes.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Planter rings.</strong> A mix of trapped moisture, tannin and mildew. Clean, brighten, and put feet under the pots afterwards.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Tree sap.</strong> Mineral spirits on a rag for the bulk of it, then wash the area normally. Do not scrape it off a finished board.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="flex items-start gap-2 text-gray-700">
              <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-[#1E90FF] mt-0.5 flex-shrink-0" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="2" d="M9 12l2 2 4-4"></path></svg>
              <span><strong>Old paint or stain drips.</strong> Chemistry, not force. A stripper on the spot, or careful sanding — a pressure washer will only fur the wood around them.</span>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">How Often to Do This</h2>
        <p>
          A full wash once a year, in spring, plus sweeping and clearing the board gaps through the season. That single annual hour is the cheapest maintenance available on a deck: pollen, dirt and early mildew sitting on a finish measurably shorten its life, and removing them pushes back the day the whole deck needs recoating.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pair the wash with the water test while you are down there. If water beads on the clean, dry boards, the finish is still working and the deck needs nothing else this year. If it soaks in, you already have the deck cleaned and half the job of recoating is done — the timing question is covered in <a href="/how-often-to-stain-a-deck/">how often to stain a deck</a>.
        </p>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Pressure Instead of Chemistry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A washer removes dirt but leaves mildew spores and the grey oxidised layer, while furring the wood surface. The cleaner does the work; the washer only rinses it away. Decks cleaned by force alone take stain unevenly and grey again within months.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the Cleaner Dry on the Boards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cleaner that dries stops working and leaves streaks that show through the stain afterwards. Work in sections small enough to finish in ten minutes, avoid direct sun, and mist the surface if it starts drying before the dwell time is up.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the Brightener</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleaners leave the wood alkaline, darker and less absorbent. Fifteen minutes with an oxalic acid brightener neutralises that, lifts iron and tannin marks and opens the grain. Skipping it is the most common cause of a blotchy stain job on a properly cleaned deck.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Staining Too Soon Afterwards</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A deck that feels dry underfoot can still hold plenty of moisture in the top few millimetres of wood. Give it 24 to 48 hours, longer in humidity or shade, and confirm with a water-drop test — stain applied over damp wood sits on the surface and stays tacky.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Plants and the Siding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cleaner runoff lands on foliage below the deck and splashes onto siding at the house wall. Wet the plants before you start and keep wetting them, and rinse siding as you go — dried cleaner residue on vinyl leaves streaks that are harder to remove than what you were cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best thing to clean a deck with?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A percarbonate (oxygen bleach) cleaner for most decks. It lifts grey, dirt and mildew without damaging wood fibres or killing planting, and it is what most commercial deck cleaners are built on. Save hypochlorite for heavy black mould, and a stripper for a failed film finish.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you clean a deck before staining?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sweep and clear the board gaps, wet the deck and the plants, apply a percarbonate cleaner and let it dwell 10 to 15 minutes without drying, scrub with the grain, rinse hard, then brighten and rinse again. Allow 24 to 48 hours of drying and confirm with a water-drop test.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you clean a deck with just a pressure washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It removes dirt but not mildew spores or the grey oxidised layer, and pressure alone furs the surface. Use 500 to 1,200 psi on softwood with a 25 or 40 degree tip, 10 to 12 inches back, always moving with the grain — and let the cleaner do the actual work.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do you need a brightener after cleaning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Before staining, yes. Cleaners leave the wood alkaline and slightly darkened, which reduces how much stain it accepts. An oxalic acid brightener neutralises that, restores colour and opens the grain — it is the step most often skipped by people whose stain later looks blotchy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long should a deck dry after cleaning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Two full days in warm, dry, breezy weather and up to four in humidity or shade. Test rather than guess: sprinkle water on several boards, and if it soaks in and darkens the wood the deck is ready. Beading means there is still moisture in the surface.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use bleach to clean a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Chlorine bleach kills mildew but breaks down lignin, leaves fibres furry, corrodes fasteners and damages planting. Diluted and rinsed hard it is a fair spot treatment for heavy black mould, but percarbonate cleaners do the same job across a whole deck without the collateral damage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you clean a composite deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A composite-safe cleaner, a soft brush and thorough rinsing. Keep pressure washing under about 1,500 psi with a wide fan — erosion marks on composite are permanent. Black spotting is usually surface mould feeding on debris in the embossed grain, not damage to the board.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you clean a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A full wash once a year, ideally in spring, plus sweeping and clearing the board gaps through the season. Pollen, dirt and early mildew sitting on a finish shorten its life, so that annual hour pushes back the day the deck needs recoating.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

