        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Let the chemistry do the work.</strong> A percarbonate cleaner, ten to fifteen minutes of dwell time and a stiff brush worked with the grain removes grey, algae and mildew that no amount of pressure will touch. Keep a washer at 500 to 1,200 psi with a wide tip and treat it as a rinsing tool.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then brighten. Fifteen minutes with an oxalic acid brightener neutralises the alkaline surface the cleaner leaves, lifts rust and tannin marks and opens the grain so stain goes on evenly. It is the cheapest step in the process and the one that most often explains a blotchy finish when it is skipped.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Before staining, give the deck 24 to 48 hours to dry and check it with water rather than by eye. And wash the deck every spring whether or not you plan to recoat — an hour a year is the cheapest maintenance a deck ever gets.
          </p>
        </div>


